WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.320
President Franklin Delano
Roosevelt is a transformative

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.360
figure in American history.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.360
He steers the country
out of the Depression

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.560
and away from isolationism.

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.240
In the summer of 1944. Allied
forces are battling across France.

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.560
and heavy bombers are striking
the Japanese home islands.

7
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.840
but forcing the Axis powers
to submit to FDR's demand.

8
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.880
unconditional surrender still
requires enormous effort

9
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.680
from America and its Allies.

10
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.760
All wars change the world.

11
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.080
but none of them changed the world
like the Second World War did.

12
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.520
Japan's on the march;
Germany's on the march.

13
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.360
No one can imagine the nightmare
they're about to unleash.

14
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.120
The most destructive
war in human history.

15
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.720
Suddenly.
the world is turned upside down.

16
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.320
and all hell is let loose.

17
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.960
The West is stunned by
the speed of the advance.

18
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.600
You get the Allies
led by the Big Three:

19
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.040
Roosevelt. Churchill. Stalin.

20
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.920
Men who are dealing with immensely
complicated questions.

21
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.600
It's the biggest military
operation of human history.

22
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.880
The Allies have to come together.
not just militarily.

23
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.920
but industrial scale.
It's a global perspective.

24
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.080
They have to fight
in every climate.

25
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.600
from the Arctic
to the jungles of the Pacific.

26
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.320
to the deserts of Africa.
and the depths of the ocean.

27
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.240
But there was no
certainty of victory.

28
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.880
It was going to be
a horrific bloodbath.

29
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.400
We see humans
at their absolute worst.

30
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.680
how they treat other human beings.

31
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.720
and we see them
at their absolute best.

32
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.080
willing to give their lives
that others might live.

33
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.080
World War II was a struggle
in which there could be one victor

34
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.880
and one vanquished.

35
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.520
<i>Summer. 1944.</i>

36
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.280
<i>While American forces
combat Germany in Europe</i>

37
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.200
<i>and Japan in the Asia Pacific.</i>

38
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.800
<i>in the United States.
it's an election year.</i>

39
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.400
<i>Even though his nation is at war.
the Commander In Chief.</i>

40
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.920
<i>the most powerful man in the world.
must seek the approval of voters.</i>

41
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.600
Franklin Roosevelt is trying to do
something no American has ever done.

42
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.640
which is be elected to
a fourth term as President.

43
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.600
and the country is not ready
to turn Franklin Roosevelt out.

44
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.720
but they're not thrilled
about rehiring him either.

45
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.240
Roosevelt wasn't in robust health

46
00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.480
when he first ran
for president in 1932.

47
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.400
He was a polio victim as a young
man; he's in a wheelchair.

48
00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.520
Those who've known him before
the war always come away a bit

49
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.760
shocked at how dramatically
he seemed to age.

50
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.280
The stress of the war
has ground him down.

51
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.680
so he's got to demonstrate to
the American people that he is still

52
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.320
firmly in command in this war and
can bring it to a successful close.

53
00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.600
Roosevelt decides
to sail over to Hawaii

54
00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.240
to hold a wartime conference
with his top leaders.

55
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.360
By being on a warship
at the site where the war started.

56
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.800
for the Americans.
he has the ability to project

57
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.040
an image of continued vitality
and strength.

58
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.680
<i>The primary purpose
of the Pearl Harbor Conference</i>

59
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.480
<i>is to determine American strategy
against Japan in the Pacific.</i>

60
00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.280
Roosevelt looks at Japan
and sees a country infected

61
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.840
with a kind of militarism...

62
00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.880
...A desire to conquer
its neighbours.

63
00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.200
a notion of racial superiority
over all the other peoples in Asia.

64
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.800
To prevent
another Pearl Harbor.

65
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.000
he's going to have to stick to
the policy he declared in 1943:

66
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.640
unconditional surrender.

67
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.120
<i>The United States
controls most of the Pacific.</i>

68
00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.480
<i>including New Guinea and the
recently captured Mariana Islands.</i>

69
00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.560
<i>The Navy wants to push on and
take Formosa. modern-day Taiwan.</i>

70
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.640
<i>as a prelude to invading Japan.</i>

71
00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.040
<i>General Douglas MacArthur
has other ideas.</i>

72
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.600
Douglas MacArthur is one of the
most famous American generals

73
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.120
in history.
also one of the most controversial.

74
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.080
He's got an ego
that is unbelievable.

75
00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.520
In Hawaii. he shows up late.

76
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.080
He keeps the President
waiting on purpose.

77
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.800
And then rolls in.
in this leather jacket.

78
00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.040
decidedly casual.
in complete contrast to the Navy.

79
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.280
which is all turned out
in their dress whites.

80
00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.720
MacArthur believes
he's more important to the country

81
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:12.960
than the President.

82
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.080
In fact. he believes
he <i>is</i> the country.

83
00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.360
that his will and his desires
in some way embody the will

84
00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.600
and desire of the American people.

85
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.720
<i>MacArthur insists American forces</i>

86
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.360
<i>must return to the Philippines for
reasons both strategic and personal.</i>

87
00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.040
Douglas MacArthur
had a long-standing affiliation

88
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.640
with the Philippines.

89
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.840
He had a real affinity
for the Filipino people.

90
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.400
He had lived there before the war.

91
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:46.120
and then in 1941 and 1942.

92
00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.040
he was the commander in chief
of that garrison

93
00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.680
when it was attacked by the Japanese
at the very beginning of the war.

94
00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.480
<i>When Japanese forces
overran the Philippines in 1942.</i>

95
00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.120
<i>Roosevelt ordered General MacArthur
to evacuate to Australia.</i>

96
00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.960
<i>He understood MacArthur's
personal value to the morale</i>

97
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.480
<i>of the American public.</i>

98
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.080
<i>After months of bitter fighting.</i>

99
00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.720
<i>American and Filipino troops
surrendered to the Japanese.</i>

100
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.520
<i>In the cruellest conditions.</i>

101
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.040
<i>they were marched
off the Bataan Peninsula</i>

102
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.160
<i>and into captivity:
the Bataan Death March.</i>

103
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.040
<i>During the Japanese occupation.</i>

104
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.840
<i>hundreds of thousands of Filipinos
are killed.</i>

105
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.680
<i>Women are forced into sex slavery;</i>

106
00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.400
<i>entire villages are destroyed
in mass executions.</i>

107
00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.000
As he is about to leave. MacArthur
makes this famous pronouncement:

108
00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.280
I shall return.

109
00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.320
It's not <i>"we</i> shall return";
it's <i>"I</i> shall return."

110
00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:57.360
This is a vow that becomes
really a rallying cry for Americans.

111
00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.880
not only in the Philippines
who are captured.

112
00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.520
but also for Americans back home.

113
00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.600
<i>At Pearl Harbor.
MacArthur presses the President</i>

114
00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:09.200
<i>to strike Japan through the
Philippines instead of Formosa.</i>

115
00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.680
The problem with the Philippines
is it's not just an island;

116
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.440
it's an archipelago.

117
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.280
And there's a very large Japanese
army on these islands.

118
00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.320
hundreds of thousands of troops.

119
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.840
That could lead to protracted
fighting with the Japanese.

120
00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.600
But MacArthur has some
arguments on his side as well.

121
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.920
The Philippines are
an American possession.

122
00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.520
They're a commonwealth.

123
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.280
To simply bypass the Philippines
and go to Taiwan

124
00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.240
will make America look
like a very inconstant partner.

125
00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.640
not to mention abandoning the
survivors of the Bataan Death March.

126
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.360
<i>Roosevelt leaves the conference
without making a decision.</i>

127
00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.920
<i>A week later. though. he sends
a letter to General MacArthur</i>

128
00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.080
<i>agreeing to his plan.</i>

129
00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.440
<i>On October 20th.</i>

130
00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.920
<i>over a hundred thousand US troops</i>

131
00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.080
<i>backed by the Seventh
and Third US Fleets</i>

132
00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.600
<i>land on Leyte. a central island
in the Philippines</i>

133
00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.360
<i>with a protected gulf.</i>

134
00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.200
<i>MacArthur's campaign
to retake the country has begun.</i>

135
00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.320
He had the Army
Signal Corps of Engineers

136
00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.520
go ahead on the beach ahead of him.

137
00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.680
so by the time he got there.
he gives his radio address.

138
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:43.120
<i>People of the Philippines.</i>

139
00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:44.600
<i>I have returned.</i>

140
00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.800
The fact that MacArthur
is able to return

141
00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:55.240
to fulfil that promise
is a morale boost.

142
00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.040
But the theatre of MacArthur saying.
"I have returned."

143
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.840
does not translate into
straightforward Allied victory.

144
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.520
The Japanese know
that General MacArthur

145
00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.280
has promised to return.

146
00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.920
and so they anticipated the next

147
00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.200
decisive battle may
be over the Philippines.

148
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.240
<i>The Philippines sit between
Japan and the Dutch East Indies.</i>

149
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.160
<i>Japan's primary source of oil.</i>

150
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.040
So if the Philippines get captured.

151
00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.040
it will cut off the supply lines.

152
00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.400
and the military runs out of oil.

153
00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.480
<i>The Japanese already have
a plan to defend the Philippines:</i>

154
00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.080
<i>Operation Sho-Go. or victory.</i>

155
00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.840
<i>They will send all their warships to
destroy the US fleets in Leyte Gulf.</i>

156
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.520
<i>then trap General MacArthur's
troops on the island.</i>

157
00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.400
The Japanese position is

158
00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.680
it's absolutely imperative to
carry out this operation now.

159
00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:02.960
We've got oil. we've got warships.
We better use them or lose them.

160
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.120
This is almost like one
last banzai charge at sea

161
00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:11.520
that can have a material effect
on the outcome of the war.

162
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.240
That's the kind of spirit

163
00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.040
in which the Japanese
are heading into this battle.

164
00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.520
The Allies have promised the
Japanese that the war's gonna go on

165
00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.720
until unconditional surrender
of the Japanese empire.

166
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.920
But deep down inside. I'm not
sure the Japanese believe that.

167
00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:30.280
They still think they can
inflict enough casualties

168
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:31.800
on the United States
that eventually.

169
00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.440
despite all the talk of
unconditional surrender.

170
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.960
the Americans
will be willing to talk.

171
00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.800
<i>As MacArthur and his troops
land on Leyte.</i>

172
00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.280
<i>the Japanese fleets move
towards the Philippines.</i>

173
00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.120
At this point in the war.

174
00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.360
the Japanese have suffered
terrible losses in the Pacific.

175
00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.240
from Midway to Guadalcanal.

176
00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.240
but they still have a tremendously
powerful fleet.

177
00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:01.280
They have these super battleships.
the Musashi and the Yamato.

178
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.880
These are the biggest battleships
ever built.

179
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.080
and this has always been
the US Navy's fear.

180
00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:13.600
If these massive Yamato-class
battleships get loose in amongst

181
00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:17.240
MacArthur's troop ships.
the invasion support vessels.

182
00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:18.720
the devastation could be immense.

183
00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.280
<i>Two Japanese fleets.</i>

184
00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:25.480
<i>a central fleet
and a southern fleet.</i>

185
00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.640
<i>will converge on Leyte Gulf.</i>

186
00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.480
<i>where US naval forces are
protecting MacArthur's landing.</i>

187
00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:33.680
<i>Success depends on a third fleet</i>

188
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.680
<i>moving towards the gulf
from the north:</i>

189
00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.680
<i>a decoy fleet.</i>

190
00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.600
There's another aspect.

191
00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.640
There's essentially a carrier task
force that sails towards Leyte Gulf

192
00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:44.840
from the north.

193
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.840
It has four aircraft carriers in it.

194
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.640
It's designed to try to
lure American ships away

195
00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.600
from protecting MacArthur.

196
00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.400
And this is the brilliance
of the Japanese move.

197
00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.440
They decide to use
the carriers as bait.

198
00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:01.440
The entire Japanese strategy
is to trap MacArthur.

199
00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.960
trap this famous general himself. potentially.

200
00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:07.800
They're gonna bring these two
heavily armed and armoured pincers

201
00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:12.160
against the American landing force
while it is still unloading cargo

202
00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:16.560
and supplies and troops
at Leyte and annihilate it.

203
00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.360
<i>General MacArthur has
landed 130.000 troops on Leyte.</i>

204
00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:36.800
<i>the first step in the American
liberation of the Philippines.</i>

205
00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.720
<i>But as US troops establish
a secure beachhead.</i>

206
00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:44.440
<i>the Japanese Navy enacts
Operation Sho-Go.</i>

207
00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.360
The Japanese have to
figure out how to sneak their forces

208
00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:50.720
close to the landing beaches
without being discovered.

209
00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:54.560
because if they come under heavy
air attack. they're in real trouble.

210
00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.640
So they plan to lure most of the
American forces out of the fight.

211
00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:04.200
<i>There are two American fleets
supporting MacArthur's forces:</i>

212
00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:08.200
<i>the US Third Fleet led
by Admiral Bull Halsey.</i>

213
00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:12.480
<i>and the Seventh led by
Admiral Thomas Kinkaid.</i>

214
00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.960
<i>As the Japanese cross
the Sibuyan Sea.</i>

215
00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.360
<i>they're spotted by one
of Halsey's scout planes.</i>

216
00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.400
The Americans
attack from the air.

217
00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.160
and their better focus was
on the largest ships they see.

218
00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.120
which was the super battleships
Yamato and Musashi.

219
00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.840
Musashi is an absolute giant.

220
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:43.440
She has 18.1-inch naval guns.
the largest naval guns ever mounted.

221
00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.240
The Japanese have even developed
a special anti-aircraft shell

222
00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.560
for them as well.

223
00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.080
which explodes in
this enormous cloud.

224
00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.640
and it's supposed to destroy
any American planes

225
00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:55.920
that are flying through it.

226
00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.440
But the attacks that are launched
by these American planes

227
00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.600
gradually start whittling away
at her defensive capabilities.

228
00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:13.000
Torpedo after torpedo
streaks towards the Musashi.

229
00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:15.440
hitting and exploding
below the waterline.

230
00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:19.120
17 to 19 torpedoes.

231
00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:20.600
more than a dozen bombs...

232
00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.040
...finally get her to go down.

233
00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:31.920
<i>The Musashi sinks
along with a thousand of her crew.</i>

234
00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.840
<i>The Japanese central fleet retreats.</i>

235
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.080
<i>That night. Kinkaid's fleet.</i>

236
00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:42.120
<i>including battleships that had
been damaged at Pearl Harbor.</i>

237
00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:45.040
<i>decimates the Japanese
southern fleet.</i>

238
00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:50.880
<i>But Halsey again spots Japanese
warships north of Leyte Gulf.</i>

239
00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:53.560
<i>including four aircraft carriers:</i>

240
00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:55.760
<i>the Japanese decoy fleet.</i>

241
00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.960
In Halsey's mind.
as a carrier commander himself.

242
00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:05.080
any enemy carrier fleet represents
the most dire threat to his mission.

243
00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.200
which is protecting
the invasion forces.

244
00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:12.680
So Halsey takes his entire fleet
and goes charging off after it.

245
00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:15.000
And so Halsey's
played into the Japanese hands

246
00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.080
exactly as they had hoped.

247
00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.800
Halsey's taken all
the major firepower north with him.

248
00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:26.640
and that puts MacArthur's invasion
at risk.

249
00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:34.960
<i>Halsey moves north.
but the Japanese central fleet</i>

250
00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:39.400
<i>under Admiral Takeo Kurita
returns to Leyte Gulf.</i>

251
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:45.160
<i>From the battleship Yamato.
Kurita sees that only a portion</i>

252
00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:48.640
<i>of the Seventh Fleet is left
to protect MacArthur's invasion.</i>

253
00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:53.000
For Kurita.
this is a heaven-sent opportunity.

254
00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:54.760
The dawn breaks.

255
00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:02.320
and now there is an American fleet
sitting directly under his guns.

256
00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.240
All he has to do is sink them.

257
00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.280
The American vessels
that are still around Leyte

258
00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:12.800
primarily provide support
for the landing forces going ashore.

259
00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:16.160
So there's only a token force
of light escort carriers

260
00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:17.560
and destroyers.

261
00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:23.000
Escort carriers are small.
extremely vulnerable.

262
00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:26.240
They can carry
25 to 30 aircraft max.

263
00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:30.280
They're designed for ground
support for the troops ashore.

264
00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:31.560
That's their role.

265
00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.560
It is not to get themselves
into stand-up fights

266
00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:37.080
against large enemy warships.

267
00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:40.640
<i>The escort carriers
launch their planes</i>

268
00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.600
<i>against Kurita's much larger fleet.</i>

269
00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.720
These escort carriers. mostly what
they do is turn around and run.

270
00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.960
because they are not designed
to fight a threat like this.

271
00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.760
<i>The only force that
can match the Japanese.</i>

272
00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.920
<i>Admiral Halsey's Third Fleet.</i>

273
00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:59.480
<i>remains a hundred miles
to the north.</i>

274
00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.720
<i>His commander in chief.
Admiral Chester Nimitz.</i>

275
00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:04.800
<i>requests his location.</i>

276
00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.920
Halsey receives
a message from Nimitz:

277
00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:13.440
"Where. repeat. where. is Task
Force 34? The world wonders."

278
00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:16.040
Traditionally. naval messages
begin and end

279
00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:19.880
with sort of a nonsense phrase
to confuse code breakers.

280
00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.560
The nonsense phrase they used
in this message. though. is

281
00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:24.440
"The world wonders."

282
00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:27.560
And "The world wonders"
line should have been removed

283
00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:30.200
by his cipher officer. but wasn't.

284
00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:33.520
I've always wondered
if Admiral Nimitz was also sticking

285
00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:36.560
a little barb in Halsey as to.
"What are you doing up there?"

286
00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:41.440
And the result is that
Halsey just goes into conniptions.

287
00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:45.240
throws his cap on the floor.
swearing up and down.

288
00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:50.160
His chief of staff has to tell him
to get a grip on this situation.

289
00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.840
<i>Halsey realises the danger.</i>

290
00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.840
<i>He sends his fastest battleships
back to Leyte Gulf. then continues</i>

291
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:01.080
<i>after the Japanese carriers.</i>

292
00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.240
Now.
what the Americans don't know

293
00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.480
is that the Japanese carriers
lying to Halsey's north

294
00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.080
don't have many planes on them.

295
00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:09.440
In fact. most of the planes
have been offloaded already

296
00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.200
and flown to the island of Luzon
for what is about to come.

297
00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.880
because a new and terrifying weapon
emerges from this battle...

298
00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:20.720
...the kamikaze.

299
00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:39.360
<i>At an airfield on Luzon.</i>

300
00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:42.800
<i>Japanese pilots are assembled
for a unique mission.</i>

301
00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:46.720
Japanese pilots.
at the beginning of the war.

302
00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:50.680
were the cream of the crop.
elite. hand-selected.

303
00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:52.560
and carefully trained.

304
00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:54.120
But by this point in the war.

305
00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:59.080
Japan just doesn't have the time or
the resources to train its pilots.

306
00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.440
The Japanese
military leadership

307
00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:08.320
has to make a tough decision
to make a cost-efficient way

308
00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:11.760
of destroying American enemy ships.

309
00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:18.160
And one way to do it is to
undertake one-way flight

310
00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:23.680
that will end with crashing a plane.

311
00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.160
<i>This new unit of 24 pilots.</i>

312
00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.800
<i>led by 23-year-old
Lieutenant Yukio Seki.</i>

313
00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:36.440
<i>is Japan's first organised
special attack unit.</i>

314
00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.240
<i>They will soon become
known as kamikaze:</i>

315
00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:41.920
<i>the divine wind.</i>

316
00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.120
So the kamikaze
in some ways represents

317
00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:50.640
a perversion of traditional Japanese
bushido doctrine. the samurai code.

318
00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:55.720
this idea of sacrificing one's life
for Japan and for the emperor.

319
00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:59.120
It's hard to generalise
about their motivations.

320
00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:02.560
You find some of them saying.
"I'm doing this for the emperor."

321
00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.680
Other pilots talk
about their village.

322
00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:09.080
they talk about families. and
they say. "I'm doing this for you."

323
00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:14.040
For the Japanese. what they're doing
was not regarded as suicide.

324
00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:15.840
It's a death in combat.

325
00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.040
So it's this sense
that these young men

326
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:20.800
are becoming something
really special.

327
00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:24.600
that almost to give this level of
self-sacrifice makes you a samurai.

328
00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:31.920
<i>In Leyte Gulf.</i>

329
00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:34.760
<i>the American escort carriers
of the US Seventh Fleet</i>

330
00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:38.080
<i>confront a new and lethal threat
from the air.</i>

331
00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.600
Stopping a kamikaze plane
is almost impossible.

332
00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:57.800
The American carriers
can only do two things.

333
00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:00.840
One is manoeuvre away.

334
00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.440
and the second is put up
a huge curtain of flak.

335
00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:11.000
But often-times the kamikazes
can break through that flak curtain.

336
00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:15.240
You could fire every
anti-aircraft gun in the world.

337
00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:19.640
but unless you get something like
a direct hit that kills the pilot.

338
00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:22.600
that plane's gonna hit your ship.

339
00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:29.200
There's a psychological
effect as well.

340
00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:32.400
Japanese kamikaze are giving up
their lives in the most violent ways

341
00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:34.560
possible simply to kill Americans.

342
00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:38.160
This is something that is difficult
for Americans to understand.

343
00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:42.200
that this is a human being
who has chosen to do this to you.

344
00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.320
How do you fight
against an enemy like this?

345
00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:48.400
<i>Lieutenant Seki crashes
his plane into the flight deck</i>

346
00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:52.840
<i>of an American escort carrier.
the USS St. Lo.</i>

347
00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:56.320
<i>It's the first ship to be sunk
by kamikaze attack.</i>

348
00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:01.240
<i>Six other vessels are struck
and barely stay afloat.</i>

349
00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:08.880
<i>But Admiral Kurita's central fleet
also faces an aerial attack.</i>

350
00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:11.800
Despite the pounding
the Americans have taken.

351
00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:15.320
it is actually Kurita who is under
a major disadvantage here.

352
00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.120
He's up against 350 American planes.

353
00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:22.440
and because Kurita
is being constantly attacked

354
00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:27.240
by American aircraft.
he becomes increasingly paranoid

355
00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:30.080
that he is now surrounded
by American carriers.

356
00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:33.680
So after these
moments of tension where Kurita

357
00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:36.880
could have potentially wreaked havoc
on the American fleet in Leyte Gulf.

358
00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.000
he actually withdraws.

359
00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:43.880
<i>Kurita retreats ahead of
Admiral Halsey's returning warships.</i>

360
00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:47.360
<i>but the Japanese have lost
a third of their navy.</i>

361
00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:50.760
It's the largest naval
battle in human history.

362
00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:53.600
More than 200.000
combatants involved.

363
00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.760
and the American victory really
knocks the Japanese Imperial Navy

364
00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:58.680
out of the war in the Pacific.

365
00:22:58.840 --> 00:22:59.960
But without a doubt.

366
00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:01.840
the Japanese are
not gonna surrender.

367
00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:04.040
This perversion of the bushido code.

368
00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.800
it has manifested itself on islands
throughout this war.

369
00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.640
and now it's manifesting
itself in the sea and in the air.

370
00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:13.520
So it's starting to get in the mind
of the Allies about how costly

371
00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:17.120
it will be to achieve that
unconditional surrender of Japan.

372
00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:22.560
<i>Over two months of fighting
in dense jungle terrain</i>

373
00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:24.600
<i>against fierce Japanese resistance.</i>

374
00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:28.360
<i>General MacArthur's troops
retake Leyte.</i>

375
00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:31.680
<i>Luzon is next.</i>

376
00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:35.000
Luzon in particular is
gonna be a very tough nut to crack.

377
00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:38.720
because not only is it a large
island with mountain chains

378
00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:40.800
the Japanese can fortify.

379
00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.240
but there's also this enormous
capital of Manila

380
00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:46.240
with about a million people
sitting in the middle of the island.

381
00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:49.760
And if the American forces get
involved in urban combat there.

382
00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.880
the casualties are likely
to be very. very heavy.

383
00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:59.040
<i>12 days after
the victory at Leyte Gulf.</i>

384
00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.520
<i>it's election night
in the United States.</i>

385
00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:04.160
<i>At Hyde Park.
a victorious President greets</i>

386
00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:06.000
<i>townspeople on election night.</i>

387
00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:09.800
<i>Franklin D Roosevelt
wins an unprecedented fourth term.</i>

388
00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:14.280
<i>The unconditional surrender
of Japan and Germany</i>

389
00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.000
<i>remains his highest priority.</i>

390
00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:26.840
<i>On January 9th. 1945.</i>

391
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.000
<i>MacArthur's troops land
on Luzon in the Philippines.</i>

392
00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:33.160
<i>Quickly. special operation forces
trek inland</i>

393
00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:35.640
<i>to liberate Japanese POW camps.</i>

394
00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:44.160
At the end of two years
of captivity in Japanese hands.

395
00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:46.120
these men are in terrible condition.

396
00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:48.440
A third of them have already died.

397
00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:54.840
There are also a number of American
civilian detainees that are in camps

398
00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:56.640
that are near Manila.

399
00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:01.280
so there's a ticking clock here
as far as MacArthur is concerned.

400
00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:05.520
He needs to liberate these
camps as rapidly as possible.

401
00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:09.720
<i>In early February.</i>

402
00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:12.200
<i>American forces surround
the capital of Manila.</i>

403
00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:14.600
<i>Most Japanese troops
have retreated north</i>

404
00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:17.200
<i>to create an extensive
defensive line.</i>

405
00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:21.400
<i>but one group stays with orders
to fight to the last man.</i>

406
00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:27.880
The real problem of urban
warfare in the modern era

407
00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:31.400
is that the city
is a gigantic sponge.

408
00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:33.800
You can have a million-man army.

409
00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:37.560
and suddenly it's split up
all over the place.

410
00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:40.840
So the Americans
find themselves having to fight

411
00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:43.000
street by street.
building by building.

412
00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:48.080
and it really turns Manila
into Stalingrad of the Pacific.

413
00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:59.160
<i>MacArthur's men are supported
by Filipino resistance fighters</i>

414
00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:02.320
<i>who provide intel.
disrupt Japanese supply lines.</i>

415
00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:05.400
<i>and help secure key routes
into the city.</i>

416
00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:09.680
There have been
active groups of Filipino guerrillas

417
00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.120
in existence since 1942.

418
00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:17.200
and those guerrillas have been
carrying on a very successful

419
00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:20.400
campaign of resistance
against the Japanese occupiers.

420
00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:28.400
The Japanese response.
like the Nazis in Europe.

421
00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:33.400
was to use guerrilla activity
as an excuse to murder civilians.

422
00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:38.680
So whether
it's a school. a hospital. homes.

423
00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:41.400
Filipino people are being bayoneted.

424
00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:45.440
they're being shot. beheaded.
sometimes burned alive.

425
00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:47.080
corpses left to rot.

426
00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:50.640
There's a sense of scorched earth.

427
00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:53.920
a sense that we will leave
nothing for the Americans.

428
00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:56.920
The Japanese
will ultimately take the lives

429
00:26:57.080 --> 00:26:59.400
of a hundred thousand
Filipino civilians.

430
00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:01.680
The Battle of Manila.
in a lot of ways.

431
00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:03.320
is a harbinger of what's to come.

432
00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:09.240
Roosevelt
and his top military advisors

433
00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:13.080
are aware of the increasingly
dire implications

434
00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:15.760
of a longer war with Japan.

435
00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:20.000
but the American people are focused
on what's going on in Europe.

436
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:23.560
and they think the war
is now winding down.

437
00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:27.200
President Roosevelt's
just been re-elected.

438
00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:28.960
He's looking at the horrors.

439
00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.360
the brutality of the fighting
in the Philippines.

440
00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:33.400
and he's been peppered
with letters from the mums

441
00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:36.040
of America saying. "Where is my boy?
What is he doing?

442
00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:38.440
What God-forsaken island
is he fighting on this time?"

443
00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:42.080
He needs to find a way
to strengthen the will and resolve

444
00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:43.600
of the American people.

445
00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:45.880
because he knows things
are going to get a lot worse

446
00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.120
before they get better.

447
00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.960
<i>Roosevelt meets with British
Prime Minister Winston Churchill</i>

448
00:27:57.120 --> 00:28:00.720
<i>in Malta to discuss the strategy
for ending the war in Europe</i>

449
00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:02.400
<i>and in the Pacific.</i>

450
00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:05.560
The question is.
if we have an enemy willing

451
00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:08.600
to sacrifice young men
in kamikaze attacks.

452
00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:12.160
an enemy that seems willing
to fight on beyond reason.

453
00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:14.480
how are we going to
bring this to an end?

454
00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:18.800
Roosevelt's advisors are arguing
that the Japanese will not give up.

455
00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:20.440
This war will not end

456
00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:24.720
until there is actually an invasion
of the home islands.

457
00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:30.080
Churchill and Roosevelt
are in Malta on the way to Yalta

458
00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:31.800
early in 1945.

459
00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:35.480
and this is where Operation Downfall
first crystallises:

460
00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:37.240
the invasion of Japan.

461
00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:42.200
The sense is that after the war
in Europe would be over.

462
00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:44.120
and all of the resources.
all the men.

463
00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:47.480
all the equipment that they could
muster would be transferred into

464
00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.920
the Far East theatre
for a massive. massive operation.

465
00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.720
the kind of which
history has never seen before.

466
00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:55.080
This would occur

467
00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:57.480
after a protracted strategic
bombing campaign.

468
00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:02.480
which as of early 1945 is beginning
to take more and more effect.

469
00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:06.760
<i>At first. the Marines
are ordered to take Iwo Jima.</i>

470
00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:13.080
Japan has
an airfield on Iwo Jima.

471
00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:17.560
and as the B-29s start operating
out of Saipan and the Marianas.

472
00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:22.320
they're using it to spot the B-29
formations on their radar scopes.

473
00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:26.760
Iwo Jima becomes
a logical place to. first of all.

474
00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:29.320
remove something
from the Japanese control.

475
00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:32.440
Second. don't allow them
to have any Japanese fighter

476
00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:34.760
on there or early warning systems.

477
00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:38.680
And third. for all those B-29s
that are making those long flights

478
00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:42.680
over 1.200 miles. it creates a place
to land if they have engine trouble.

479
00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:44.800
if they run into any difficulties.

480
00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:46.280
So Iwo Jima has gotta go.

481
00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:50.320
That needs to become
an American airfield.

482
00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:54.560
American intelligence tells
the Marines that there are about

483
00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:56.240
13.000 Japanese defenders.

484
00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.560
but there are actually 23.000.

485
00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:04.840
nearly double what the intelligence
estimates listed.

486
00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:08.920
As a result.
you have an American invasion force

487
00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:13.200
that's going to be meeting a much
tougher defence than it expected.

488
00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:33.640
<i>Japanese troops on Iwo Jima</i>

489
00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:37.960
<i>have been preparing for an American
attack for over three months.</i>

490
00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:42.000
The Japanese strategy
is to make the battle

491
00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:44.160
last as long as possible.

492
00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:46.600
Each day that that battle lasts

493
00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:48.560
is a day where the people
at the home islands

494
00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:51.120
are spared of an American
invasion.

495
00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:55.440
So the commander of the Japanese
on Iwo Jima. Kuribayashi.

496
00:30:55.600 --> 00:30:59.320
says we're going to prepare these
defensive fortifications and wait.

497
00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:02.440
draw the Americans in.
and then attack them.

498
00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:08.400
The Japanese aren't on Iwo
Jima; they're actually in Iwo Jima.

499
00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:13.160
They have miles and miles
of tunnels that they have dug

500
00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:17.000
out of this soft volcanic terrain
on the island.

501
00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:21.920
And they've also fortified the most
prominent landmark on Iwo Jima.

502
00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:23.920
which is Mount Suribachi.

503
00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:27.520
<i>Looming over the
Marines' landing beaches.</i>

504
00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:30.200
<i>Mount Suribachi provides
the Japanese</i>

505
00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:32.760
<i>with a perfect defensive position.</i>

506
00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:36.200
It's gonna be this
defensive hive that the Americans

507
00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:39.200
will have to reduce cave by cave.

508
00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:41.920
bunker by bunker.
one thing at a time.

509
00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:47.000
<i>On Iwo Jima. Japanese
soldiers are issued orders to kill</i>

510
00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:48.720
<i>10 Americans before they die.</i>

511
00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:54.360
We see very few Japanese
who attempt to surrender.

512
00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:57.720
There's a view that this is a great
dishonour. not only to oneself

513
00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.560
and one's family.
but to the emperor as well.

514
00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:05.360
{\an8}If you look at
the letters that Kuribayashi writes

515
00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:07.760
{\an8}home to his family.
he's clear right from the beginning

516
00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:09.480
that no one on the Japanese side

517
00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:11.840
is coming home
from the Iwo Jima campaign.

518
00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:15.240
He starts to issue these orders
very clearly to his men saying.

519
00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:17.720
"Fight to the death."
but he doesn't want a banzai charge.

520
00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:20.960
He doesn't want pointless
suicidal acts.

521
00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:24.440
He wants to show the Americans
the cost to them

522
00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:26.760
of going as far as the Japanese
home islands.

523
00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:38.800
<i>The Navy bombarded
Japanese positions for three days.</i>

524
00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:46.520
<i>Now the Marines plan to land
60.000 men across Iwo Jima's</i>

525
00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:50.640
<i>southern beaches. push north.
and take the island.</i>

526
00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:55.360
So at 8:59
on the 19th of February.

527
00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:57.720
the first 10.000 men land.

528
00:32:57.880 --> 00:32:59.840
and it's quiet.

529
00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:04.400
But then at 10 o'clock
in the morning...

530
00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:09.840
...every Japanese soldier
with a mortar. machine gun.

531
00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.960
or a piece of artillery will now
start to shell the landing beach.

532
00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:25.400
<i>In the face of withering fire.</i>

533
00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:28.720
<i>the Marines struggle up dunes
of 15 feet or more.</i>

534
00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:35.720
Iwo Jima is not sand;
it's volcanic ash.

535
00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.280
Your feet just sink
up to your knees.

536
00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:40.520
It's like walking in coffee grounds.

537
00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:46.640
It's incredibly deadly
to try and move forward.

538
00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:52.160
<i>Bulldozers are called in
to clear a path through the dunes.</i>

539
00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:54.920
<i>allowing the Marines
to continue their advance.</i>

540
00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:59.760
As the Marines finally
make their way off the beaches.

541
00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.640
they're getting attacked
from everywhere.

542
00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:03.160
people coming out of tunnels.

543
00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:04.960
of mortars coming off
of Mount Suribachi.

544
00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:06.960
of hidden artillery emplacements
all over the place

545
00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.880
with snipers' firing positions
they can't even see.

546
00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:14.640
Individual soldier has to
somehow get up close with a grenade

547
00:34:14.800 --> 00:34:17.360
that he can put through
the firing slit

548
00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:18.920
and kill the crew that's in there.

549
00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:24.440
Ultimately. one of the
greatest weapons that the Marines

550
00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:26.920
have is what they'd call
Zippo tanks.

551
00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:30.120
which are tank-sized flamethrowers

552
00:34:31.120 --> 00:34:33.200
that can shoot long distances.

553
00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:38.200
and that becomes the weapon
of choice for Marines on Iwo Jima.

554
00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:50.120
<i>By the end of the first day.
the Marines have secured an airfield</i>

555
00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:53.040
<i>and prepared to attack
Mount Suribachi.</i>

556
00:34:54.680 --> 00:34:58.440
On Iwo Jima.
two strategies come into conflict.

557
00:34:58.600 --> 00:35:00.800
One says. no surrender.
that's the Japanese.

558
00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:04.440
and then unconditional surrender.
and that's the American position.

559
00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:09.160
And on Suribachi. the Americans
make a determined statement

560
00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:11.800
that it's going to be
unconditional surrender.

561
00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:15.440
There is no position on planet Earth
held by the Japanese army

562
00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:18.200
that American troops cannot take.

563
00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:28.240
<i>After securing the beaches.</i>

564
00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:32.040
<i>US Marines on Iwo Jima
attack Mount Suribachi.</i>

565
00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:36.800
The force that goes up the
hill fights off Japanese resistance.

566
00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:41.680
and upon reaching the summit.

567
00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:45.680
they find a water pipe that had been
associated with a Japanese cistern.

568
00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:49.280
They attach a small American
flag to that water pipe.

569
00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:51.200
and then they raise that flag.

570
00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:57.040
But they
realise the flag's too small.

571
00:35:57.200 --> 00:35:58.240
We need a bigger flag.

572
00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:02.800
So what they're gonna do is
they're gonna take a ship's flag

573
00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:06.000
and they're gonna send it up there.
And about noon. it gets up there.

574
00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:09.600
<i>Six Marines plant
the second American flag.</i>

575
00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:14.440
<i>Joe Rosenthal. an Associated Press
photographer. captures the moment.</i>

576
00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:19.600
All the ships offshore
begin blowing their horns.

577
00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:24.520
Everyone on the island can see it.

578
00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:33.760
It's a snapshot in time.

579
00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.840
The composition's nearly perfect.

580
00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:38.240
although he basically shoots it
off his hip without even using

581
00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:40.280
his rangefinder as he did it.

582
00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.200
You can't see anybody's face.

583
00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:45.280
And so really it becomes
everybody's picture.

584
00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:51.080
<i>Two days later. Rosenthal's
photograph is on the front page</i>

585
00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:53.720
<i>of newspapers across America.</i>

586
00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:57.400
<i>President Roosevelt immediately
understands how inspirational</i>

587
00:36:57.560 --> 00:36:59.240
<i>this single image is.</i>

588
00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:02.640
FDR was always
a frustrated journalist.

589
00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:05.120
He had been the editor
of the Harvard newspaper.

590
00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:08.280
He understood visual imagery.

591
00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:14.040
It's American determination
embodied in a single photograph.

592
00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:17.160
They're all working together.

593
00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:19.720
and so it tells a national story.

594
00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:23.040
It's not just those guys on top
of the hill that raised the flag.

595
00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:25.680
It's a nation that put that flag
on top of the hill.

596
00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:31.960
But it's striking that
the flag isn't fully planted.

597
00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:35.480
It's as if the struggle
is still unfolding.

598
00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:39.400
and that fit FDR's mood
at that point.

599
00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:40.960
This isn't over.

600
00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:44.040
We're getting closer.
but we haven't gotten there yet.

601
00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:48.280
<i>To FDR. these six Marines
are the perfect symbol</i>

602
00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:50.640
<i>of America's wartime effort.</i>

603
00:37:50.800 --> 00:37:52.800
He calls the Marines and says.
"Find me these guys.

604
00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:54.640
I want to bring them
to the Oval Office.

605
00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:56.720
I want to send them
on a war bond drive."

606
00:37:56.880 --> 00:37:59.920
So the Marine Corps
sends somebody to the island.

607
00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:03.280
but they find that three men who
were in the photo were dead already.

608
00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:05.720
They died within days
of the photograph being taken.

609
00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:12.720
<i>US Marines suffered
26.000 dead. wounded. and missing.</i>

610
00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:18.520
<i>Almost every Japanese
soldier on Iwo Jima is killed.</i>

611
00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:22.560
Kuribayashi himself.
who never believed

612
00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:24.520
he was getting off Iwo Jima alive.

613
00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:26.920
he retreats.
and he takes his own life.

614
00:38:29.120 --> 00:38:32.080
It's the only battle in the
Pacific where the Americans actually

615
00:38:32.240 --> 00:38:34.560
take more casualties
than the Japanese take.

616
00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:36.160
So for American military planners.

617
00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:37.880
the lesson of Iwo Jima
seems pretty clear.

618
00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:40.000
that if you're going to launch
Operation Downfall.

619
00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:42.520
if you're actually going to invade
the Japanese home islands.

620
00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:46.040
the cost is going to be immense.
because at this point in the war.

621
00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:49.960
most Americans have concluded that
Japan is never going to surrender.

622
00:38:52.520 --> 00:38:57.160
<i>After victory on Iwo Jima. the
Marine Corps sends three surviving</i>

623
00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:59.120
<i>flag raisers back to Washington.</i>

624
00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:01.760
<i>but they will not meet
their president.</i>

625
00:39:06.040 --> 00:39:07.680
<i>Ladies and gentlemen.</i>

626
00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:10.320
<i>the tragic news of the President's
death came to Washington today</i>

627
00:39:10.480 --> 00:39:12.840
<i>as a complete shock.
except to a very few people</i>

628
00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:14.600
<i>who knew of his condition.</i>

629
00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:22.800
Franklin Roosevelt
had loomed so large for so long.

630
00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:25.160
An entire generation of Americans

631
00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:27.880
really only knew him
as their president.

632
00:39:28.760 --> 00:39:31.000
Supposedly during
the funeral procession.

633
00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:34.480
a man is so overcome by his emotions

634
00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:37.760
and another man says.
"Did you know the President?"

635
00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:42.320
And he says. "No. but he knew me."

636
00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:45.120
and that's how people felt
about Franklin Roosevelt.

637
00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:49.360
Roosevelt had been
a very. very active President.

638
00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:54.360
a very. very powerful personality.
the kind of caretaker to the people.

639
00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:58.160
and he knew you can't fight
an endless war if you want

640
00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:00.160
to maintain your democratic ideals.

641
00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:04.880
He also knew how difficult
unconditional surrender

642
00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:07.600
can be to put into actual practice.

643
00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:11.720
<i>During years
of fear and uncertainty.</i>

644
00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:13.960
<i>Roosevelt was uniquely able to lead</i>

645
00:40:14.120 --> 00:40:16.720
<i>America's colossal
fight against fascism.</i>

646
00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:20.600
<i>Now. this job will fall to his
vice president. Harry Truman.</i>

647
00:40:24.520 --> 00:40:28.080
Mrs Roosevelt summons
Truman to the White House.

648
00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:31.760
He says. "Is there anything
we can do for you?"

649
00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:36.080
And Mrs Roosevelt says. "No. Harry.
is there anything we can do for you?

650
00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:39.160
For you are the one in trouble now."

651
00:40:41.720 --> 00:40:45.880
On assuming the presidency.
Truman told reporters.

652
00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:47.600
"I felt like the moon. the stars.

653
00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:50.600
and all the planets
had fallen on me."

654
00:40:50.760 --> 00:40:53.840
Harry Truman of Missouri
is now responsible

655
00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:58.600
for the successful conclusion of the
largest conflict in human history.

656
00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:02.120
including the final destruction
of Adolf Hitler's Third Reich.

657
00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:03.960
